    "slug": "igad-and-aussom-warn-funding-shortfalls-threaten-somalia-mission",
    "summary": "IGAD and AUSSOM leaders express concern over financial and logistical challenges, urging sustained international support for the mission in Somalia.",
    "content": "The Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD) and the leadership of the African Union Support and Stabilization Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM) have raised concerns about financial and logistical obstacles facing the mission. In a joint appeal, IGAD called on the United Nations and international partners to provide predictable and sustainable funding. The warning comes amid growing uncertainty over the mission's ability to sustain its operations.\n\nAUSSOM Force Commander General Sam Kavuma highlighted that the United Nations Support Office in Somalia (UNSOS) supplies approximately 85% of the mission's logistical support. He cautioned that any reduction in this support, without alternative sources, could significantly impact operations and the protection of key locations. The mission relies heavily on this support to carry out its mandate effectively.\n\nThis development follows ongoing discussions about the future of peacekeeping efforts in Somalia. AUSSOM, which replaced the previous African Union Mission in Somalia (AMISOM), has been operational since 2022. The mission's role includes supporting Somali security forces and protecting civilians. However, persistent funding gaps have raised questions about its long-term viability.\n\nThe funding shortfall matters greatly for the Somali diaspora, as many have family members in Somalia who depend on the relative stability provided by these security operations. A weakened mission could lead to increased insecurity, potentially affecting remittances and the ability of diaspora members to invest in or visit their homeland. The diaspora has long been a vital source of support for Somalia's economy and development.\n\nIGAD's appeal underscores the need for sustained international engagement in Somalia. Without adequate resources, the mission's ability to maintain security and support state-building efforts is at risk. International partners are urged to step up their contributions to ensure the mission can continue its work. The situation remains fluid, and further discussions are expected in the coming weeks.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/17/igad-iyo-aussom-oo-ka-digay-maalgelin-yari-carqaladeyn-karta-howlgalka-soomaaliya/",
